L000051	
L000052	\begin{abstract}
L000053	In ordinary solids, nonlinear optical responses are typically studied in terms of unit-cell averages due to the {\aa}ngstr\"om-scale lattice constants. In contrast, moir\'e superlattices, characterized by a large length scale, unlock an often-overlooked degree of freedom: intra-supercell spatial variations of local observables.
L000054	Here, we formulate the second-order direct current (DC) charge response in a spatially resolved manner, showing that even uniform optical illumination can drive a static, spatially non-uniform charge redistribution within a supercell. This effect is ubiquitous and cannot be forbidden by any crystalline symmetries.
L000055	Furthermore, we identify a dominant contribution arising from diverging analytical response coefficients, which leads to linear-in-time growth of the redistribution in the absence of relaxation.
L000056	This growth is driven by the convergence or divergence of local DC photocurrents.
L000057	Applying our theory to twisted bilayer MoTe$_2$, we demonstrate strong, highly tunable charge modulation controlled by light intensity and frequency, opening a route to \textit{in situ, all-optical} control of moir\'e-periodic electrostatic potentials. Our work underscores the importance of intra-cell degrees of freedom, which enable a qualitatively richer class of nonlinear optical responses in moir\'e superlattices.
L000058	\end{abstract}
L000059	
L000060	\maketitle
L000061	
L000062	{\it Introduction.---}Two-dimensional (2D) moir\'e superlattices feature a small energy scale manifested in their flat minibands \cite{bistritzer2011moire,andrei2020graphene,balents2020superconductivity,andrei2021marvels,mak2022semiconductor,kennes2021moire}. By quenching kinetic energy and amplifying the effect of electron-electron interactions, these flat bands host a rich array of correlated and topological phases, including magnetism, superconductivity, charge-ordered states, and both integer and fractional quantum anomalous Hall insulators
L000063	\cite{andrei2020graphene,balents2020superconductivity,andrei2021marvels,mak2022semiconductor,kennes2021moire,zhao2025exploring,cao2018unconventional,cao2018correlated,yankowitz2019tuning,lu2019superconductors,stepanov2020untying,sharpe2019emergent,xu2020correlated,huang2021correlated,jiang2019charge,serlin2020intrinsic,li2021quantum,cai2023signatures,park2023observation,zeng2023thermodynamic,xu2023observation,lu2024fractional,xie2024tunable,wang2024fractional,wang2025higher}.
L000064	
L000065	
L000066	
L000067	
L000068	
L000069	
L000070	
L000071	The small energy scale follows from the large length scale of moir\'e superlattices. Despite rapid advances in flat band physics, the direct physical implications of this large moir\'e length scale remain underexplored.
L000072	In ordinary solids, the {\aa}ngstr\"om-scale lattice constants render spatial variations of physical quantities within a unit cell experimentally undetectable.
L000073	In contrast, moir\'e lattice constants exceed those of the monolayers by orders of magnitude. Local observables --- such as charge density \cite{baeumer2015ferroelectrically,zhang2025plasmonic},
L000074	current density \cite{hu2023light,agarwal2024shift}, and polarization
L000075	\cite{kim2024electrostatic,ding2024engineering,yasuda2021stacking,zhang2023visualizing,li2025unusual} --- can exhibit pronounced spatial inhomogeneities across the supercell, accessible to direct experimental probes. These inhomogeneities represent new degrees of freedom,  inviting systematic studies of their dynamics, manipulation methods, and coupling to external fields.
L000076	
L000077	
L000078	
L000079	
L000080	
L000081	
L000082	
L000083	
L000084	\begin{figure}[t]
L000085	\centering
L000086	\includegraphics[width = \columnwidth]{figures/concept.eps}
L000087	\caption{Illustration of a moir\'e supercell under uniform optical illumination, showing in-plane, non-uniform DC photocurrents (green arrows) converging toward the cell center. Red and blue colors mark regions of positive and negative charge accumulation resulting from photocurrent convergence and divergence.}
L000088	\label{fig:concept}
L000089	\end{figure}
L000090	
L000091	In this work, we investigate nonlinear optical responses of intra-cell degrees of freedom, focusing on the second-order DC intra-cell charge response.
L000092	We show that uniform light at normal incidence induces static charge redistribution, leading to pronounced intra-cell charge variations. This effect is ubiquitous and cannot be forbidden by any crystalline symmetries.
L000093	Within the independent-particle approximation, we derive analytical expressions and identify a first-order divergence in the response coefficients,
L000094	indicating that the charge density grows linearly in time in the absence of relaxation.
L000095	This behavior stems from a non-zero divergence of the DC photocurrent.
L000096	To capture long-time saturation behavior, we introduce a phenomenological relaxation time $\uptau$, which determines the steady-state charge density.
L000097	As an example, we apply our theory to twisted bilayer MoTe$_2$ (tMoTe$_2$),
L000098	showing that the resulting charge pattern is highly tunable via light frequency. The response scales linearly with light intensity, enabling enhancement via stronger illumination.
L000099	Our results reveal a mechanism
L000100	to generate and control moir\'e-periodic electrostatic potentials by light, offering a platform for \textit{in-situ, all-optical} control of correlated phenomena in layered materials. Our work underscores the importance of intra-cell degrees of freedom, which enable a qualitatively richer class of nonlinear optical responses in moir\'e superlattices.
L000101	
L000102	{\it General theory.---}We begin by demonstrating how the optically rectified photocurrents~\cite{sipe2000second,boyd2008nonlinear,chen2022basic} inevitably lead to non-uniform charge responses, highlighting their ubiquity and motivating a spatially resolved investigation.
L000103	Consider a twisted van der Waals bilayer under spatially uniform illumination. Even though the driving electric field is homogeneous, the resulting DC photocurrent exhibits spatial modulation across the moir\'e supercell.
L000104	Crucially, no symmetry or physical constraint enforces a divergence-free DC photocurrent.
L000105	By the continuity equation $\partial_t \rho + \nabla \cdot \mathbf{j} = 0$, where $\rho$ is the charge density and $\mathbf{j}$ is the photocurrent density,
L000106	any non-zero divergence leads to local charge accumulation over time (see Fig.~\hyperref[fig:concept]{1}). This mechanism explains how uniform light can generate inhomogeneous charge distributions.
L000107	
L000108	The above analysis applies in the intermediate regime --- longer than the optical cycle but shorter than the relaxation time $\uptau$ --- before reaching steady state.
L000109	In this regime, the transient~\cite{belinicher1982kinetic,sturman2020ballistic,alexandradinata2023anomalous} DC photocurrent can exhibit non-zero divergence, driving charge inhomogeneity.
L000110	On longer timescales, charge accumulation saturates at a level determined by $\uptau$. Thus, charge inhomogeneity can be exceptionally large in high-quality samples with large $\uptau$.
L000111	
L000112	\begin{figure}[b]
L000113	    \centering
L000114	\includegraphics[width = \columnwidth]{figures/tMoTe2.eps}
L000115	\caption{(a) Band structure of $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$ at twist angle $\theta=1.0^\circ$. The $\pm \mathbf{K}$ valley degrees of
L000116	freedom, inherited from monolayer $\text{MoTe}_{\text{2}}$, remain decoupled. Blue solid and red dashed lines are bands from the $+\mathbf{K}$ and $-\mathbf{K}$ valleys, respectively.
L000117	The band gap is $1.057$~eV.
L000118	(b) Brillouin zones of the top (solid gray), bottom (dashed gray) monolayer MoTe$_2$, and the moir\'e Brillouin zone of tMoTe$_2$ (black).
L000119	(c) Enlarged moir\'e Brillouin zone, showing high-symmetry points and six reciprocal lattice vectors ($\mathbf{G}_{1-6}$) with the smallest magnitude.}
L000120	\label{fig:tMoTe2}
L000121	\end{figure}
L000122	
L000123	Consider 2D moir\'e superlattices illuminated by normally incident monochromatic light of frequency $\omega_0$. The
L000124	electric field can be written as $\mathbf{E}(t) = \mathbf{E}_0(\omega_0) \, e^{-i \omega_0 t} + \mathbf{E}_0(-\omega_0) \, e^{i \omega_0 t}$, where $\mathbf{E}_0(-\omega_0) = \mathbf{E}^{*}_0(\omega_0)$. Without illumination, the charge density is denoted as $\rho_{\text{dark}}(\mathbf{r})$; under illumination it changes by $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})$. At second order in $\mathbf{E}$, the static response of $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})$ is
L000125	\begin{equation}
L000126	\label{eq:definition}
L000127	    \Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}} = \zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) \; \mathrm{E}^{\alpha_1}_0(\omega_0)
L000128	    \mathrm{E}^{\alpha_2}_0(-\omega_0),
L000129	\end{equation}
L000130	where $\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}$ is the Fourier
L000131	component of $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})$, defined as $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r}) = \sum_{\mathbf{G}} \; \Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}} \, e^{i \mathbf{G} \cdot \mathbf{r}}$. Here, $\mathbf{G}$ is a moir\'e reciprocal lattice vector, and responses at wavevectors $\mathbf{q}\ne\mathbf{G}$ vanish due to discrete translational symmetry. In addition, the uniform component $\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}=\mathbf{0}}$ vanishes by charge conservation. In Eq.~(\ref{eq:definition}), $\alpha_1,\alpha_2 \in \{x,y\}$, and repeated indices imply summation. The response coefficient $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$
L000132	is analogous to the second-order conductivity tensor $\sigma^{\beta \alpha_1 \alpha_2}$ in photocurrent responses~\cite{sipe2000second,boyd2008nonlinear,chen2022basic}.
L000133	
L000134	In nonlinear optics, second-order responses in centrosymmetric systems are commonly regarded as vanishing~\cite{baltz1981theory,boyd2008nonlinear}. However, this applies only to spatially averaged quantities, such as polarization or current averaged over the unit cell.
L000135	In contrast, we are interested in spatially resolved charge modulations within the moir\'e supercell, which are not symmetry-forbidden.
L000136	Even with inversion symmetry, the only constraint on $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ is
L000137	$\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) = \zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(-\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$, permitting non-zero responses.
L000138	More generally, any point-group symmetry operation $\hat{g}$ imposes
L000139	\begin{equation}
L000140	    \zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\hat{g} \, \mathbf{G}; \omega_0) = g^{\alpha_1 \beta_1} \;
L000141	    \zeta^{\beta_1 \beta_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) \; g^{\alpha_2 \beta_2},
L000142	\end{equation}
L000143	where $\hat{g}$ acts on vectors as $v^{\alpha} \to g^{\alpha \beta} v^{\beta}$. These symmetry relations constrain the spatial structure of the responses without forcing it to vanish.
L000144	
L000145	Within the independent-particle approximation~\footnote{The formation of a spatially modulated charge distribution under uniform illumination is rooted in the continuity equation and is therefore fully general, persisting even in the presence of excitonic effects and other interactions beyond the independent-particle approximation.}, $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ is derived analytically via perturbation theory.
L000146	The optical field enters through the length gauge \cite{peres2017,peres2018}, as a dipole term $e\,\mathbf{E}(t)\cdot\mathbf{r}$,
L000147	with the elementary charge $e>0$. Detailed steps are given in the Supplemental Material (SM) \cite{supp}\nocite{bradlyn2024uniformity}, Sec.~\ref{sec:derivation}.
L000148	The final result is
L000149	\begin{eqnarray}
L000150	\label{eq:zeta}
L000151	    \zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) &&= e^3 \int \left[ \mathrm{d}\mathbf{k} \right] \sum_{s,s^{\prime}}
L000152	    F^{\mathbf{G}}_{\mathbf{k} s^{\prime} s} \frac{1}{2i\hbar\eta-\Delta\epsilon_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}}} \nonumber\\
L000153	    &&\times\left[ \mathrm{D}^{\alpha_2}_{\mathbf{k}},\frac{1}{\hbar\omega_0-\Delta\epsilon_{\mathbf{k}}+i\hbar\eta}
L000154	    \circ[\mathrm{D}^{\alpha_1}_{\mathbf{k}}, \mathcal{P}^{[0]}_{\mathbf{k}}] \right]_{s s^{\prime}} \nonumber\\
L000155	    &&+ (\alpha_1,\omega_0 \leftrightarrow \alpha_2,-\omega_0),
L000156	\end{eqnarray}
L000157	where $\int \left[ \mathrm{d}\mathbf{k} \right] \equiv \int_{\text{MBZ}} \frac{\mathrm{d}\mathbf{k}}{(2\pi)^2}$ denotes moir\'e Brillouin zone integration,
L000158	and the summation runs over bands $s,\,s^{\prime}$.
L000159	$\Delta\epsilon_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}} \equiv \epsilon_{\mathbf{k} s} - \epsilon_{\mathbf{k} s^{\prime}}$ is the band energy difference, and $\eta=0^+$ denotes positive infinitesimal.
L000160	$F^{\mathbf{G}}_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}} \equiv \left\langle u_{\mathbf{k} s} \middle| u_{\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{G} s^{\prime}} \right\rangle$ is
L000161	the overlap between periodic parts of Bloch states. $\mathrm{D}^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}} \equiv \delta_{s s^{\prime}} \nabla^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{k}} -
L000162	i \xi^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}}$ is the covariant derivative \cite{peres2017,peres2018}, with Berry connection
L000163	$\xi^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}} \equiv i \left\langle u_{\mathbf{k} s} \right| \nabla^{\alpha}_{\mathbf{k}} \left| u_{\mathbf{k} s^{\prime}} \right\rangle$ . $\mathcal{P}^{[0]}_{\mathbf{k} s s^{\prime}} \equiv f_{\mathbf{k} s} \delta_{s s^{\prime}}$, with $f_{\mathbf{k} s}$ the Fermi-Dirac distribution.
L000164	We use compact matrix notations for band indices: $[A,B] \equiv AB-BA$ (commutator), and
L000165	$(A\circ B)_{s s^{\prime}} \equiv A_{s s^{\prime}} B_{s s^{\prime}}$ (Hadamard product).
L000166	
L000167	In Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta}), terms with $s=s^{\prime}$ yield an $\mathcal{O}(\eta^{-1})$ divergence, isolated as (see SM \cite{supp}, Sec.~\ref{sec:derivation})
L000168	\begin{eqnarray}
L000169	\label{eq:zeta_inj}
L000170	    \zeta_{\mathrm{accum}}^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) =&& \frac{\pi e^3}{\hbar\eta}
L000171	    \int \left[ \mathrm{d}\mathbf{k} \right] \sum_{r \ne s} \left( F^{\mathbf{G}}_{\mathbf{k} ss} - F^{\mathbf{G}}_{\mathbf{k} rr} \right) \nonumber\\
L000172	    \times&& \xi^{\alpha_1}_{\mathbf{k} rs} \xi^{\alpha_2}_{\mathbf{k} sr} \Delta f_{\mathbf{k} sr} \delta(\hbar\omega_0 - \Delta\epsilon_{\mathbf{k} rs}),
L000173	\end{eqnarray}
L000174	where $\Delta f_{\mathbf{k} sr} \equiv f_{\mathbf{k} s} - f_{\mathbf{k} r}$. The subscript ``accum'' denotes photocurrent accumulation, as justified below.
L000175	Equation~(\ref{eq:zeta}) is general, but here Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta_inj}) assumes a semiconductor at zero temperature for simplicity.
L000176	Such divergences in nonlinear optical response coefficients are well known \cite{fregoso2018jerk,fregoso2019bulk,ventura2020second,gao2021intrinsic}.
L000177	For instance, injection currents \cite{sipe2000second,chan2017photocurrents,de2017quantized,zhang2019switchable,gao2021intrinsic} grow linearly in time in the absence of relaxation, corresponding to an $\mathcal{O}(\eta^{-1})$ divergence in the second-order optical conductivity $\sigma^{\beta \alpha_1 \alpha_2}$.
L000178	Jerk currents \cite{fregoso2018jerk,fregoso2019bulk,ventura2020second}, with constant second-order time derivatives and $\mathcal{O}(\eta^{-2})$ divergences in $\sigma^{\beta \alpha_1 \alpha_2 \alpha_3}$, constitute another example.
L000179	Similar to the discussion in Refs.~\cite{fregoso2018jerk,fregoso2019bulk,ventura2020second,xie2025photon,gao2021intrinsic}, such divergences imply a finite, constant time derivative of the charge response:
L000180	\begin{equation}
L000181	\label{eq:linear_growth}
L000182	    \frac{\mathrm{d}\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}}{\mathrm{d} t} = 2\eta\, \zeta_{\mathrm{accum}}^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0) \,
L000183	    \mathrm{E}_0^{\alpha_1}(\omega_0) \, \mathrm{E}_0^{\alpha_2}(-\omega_0).
L000184	\end{equation}
L000185	Equation~(\ref{eq:linear_growth}) holds only without relaxation---i.e., in the time window $1/\omega_0 \ll t \ll \uptau$.
L000186	For $t \gtrsim \uptau$, $\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}$ saturates to a value determined by $\uptau$.
L000187	Introducing relaxation processes phenomenologically by replacing $\eta\rightarrow1/\uptau$ \cite{peres2018,ventura2020second,zhang2019switchable,gao2021intrinsic}, which is
L000188	formally equivalent to switching on the external field adiabatically, the saturated charge density is given by
L000189	$\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}} = \frac{\uptau}{2} \frac{\mathrm{d}\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}}{\mathrm{d} t}$,
L000190	which is proportional to both $\uptau$ and the time derivative in Eq.~(\ref{eq:linear_growth}). In high-quality samples with large $\uptau$,
L000191	$\zeta_{\mathrm{accum}}^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ dominates the total response $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$.
L000192	We illustrate this by numerically evaluating the full and accumulation-only charge responses for the tMoTe$_2$ model, whose electronic structure is shown in Fig.~\hyperref[fig:tMoTe2]{2};
L000193	the resulting curves are shown in Fig.~\hyperref[fig:frequency_dependence]{3}, with a detailed discussion provided later.
L000194	
L000195	\begin{figure}[t]
L000196	\centering
L000197	\includegraphics[width = \columnwidth]{figures/frequency_dependence.eps}
L000198	\caption{Frequency dependence of $\zeta^{xx}(\mathbf{G}_2; \omega_0)$. Real and imaginary parts are shown in the top and bottom subplots, respectively. Here, $\zeta_0 \approx 1.602\times 10^{-19} \, \mathrm{C}^3/\mathrm{J}^2$.
L000199	The full charge response [Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta})] is shown as solid green lines; the accumulation-only contribution
L000200	[Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta_inj})] is indicated by dashed orange lines. Vertical dotted lines mark the band gap energy at
L000201	$\hbar \omega_0 \approx 1.057~\text{eV}$}
L000202	\label{fig:frequency_dependence}
L000203	\end{figure}
L000204	
L000205	\begin{figure*}
L000206	    \centering
L000207	\includegraphics[width = \textwidth]{figures/patterns.eps}
L000208	\caption{Real-space charge redistribution in $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$ under linearly polarized illumination.
L000209	(a,b) Photoinduced charge density $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})$ under $x$-polarized light at photon energies $\hbar \omega_0 = 1.104 \, \mathrm{eV}$ and
L000210	$1.122 \, \mathrm{eV}$, respectively, with fixed intensity $1.0\times 10^{11} \, \mathrm{W/m^2}$. The dashed parallelogram in (a) outlines a moir\'e supercell, with high-symmetry stacking sites MM, XM, and MX marked by black, blue, and green dots.
L000211	(c,d) Moir\'e-periodic electrostatic potentials $U(\mathbf{r})$ induced in a nearby layered material, computed from the charge distributions in (a,b), respectively. (e) Comparison between the charge response coefficient $2\eta\,\zeta^{xx}(\mathbf{G}_2; \omega_0)$ computed directly (solid pink) and inferred from the continuity equation [Eq.~(\ref{eq:CE_coef})]
L000212	via the corresponding photocurrent (cyan dots).
L000213	(f) Spatial distribution of the DC photocurrent (black arrows) and time derivative $\partial_t \Delta\rho$ (color map) under the same conditions as in (b), within a single moir\'e supercell.}
L000214	\label{fig:patterns}
L000215	\end{figure*}
L000216	
L000217	Before concluding this section, we provide a real-space interpretation of the constant time derivative of $\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}$ in
L000218	Eq.~(\ref{eq:linear_growth}): it originates from the non-zero divergence of the non-uniform DC photocurrent, which acts as a local charge source.
L000219	By local charge conservation, the charge and current responses are related via the continuity equation,
L000220	$\partial_t \Delta\rho + \nabla \cdot \mathbf{j} = 0$. Defining $\mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{G}}$ as the Fourier component of the DC photocurrent
L000221	$\mathbf{j}(\mathbf{r}) = \sum_{\mathbf{G}} \; \mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{G}} \, e^{i \mathbf{G} \cdot \mathbf{r}}$, the equation in momentum space becomes:
L000222	\begin{equation}
L000223	\label{eq:continuity_equation}
L000224	    \frac{\mathrm{d}\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}}{\mathrm{d} t} + i \, \mathbf{G} \cdot \mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{G}} = 0,
L000225	\end{equation}
L000226	where $\mathbf{G}$ runs over moir\'e reciprocal lattice vectors. According to Eq.~(\ref{eq:continuity_equation}), if
L000227	$i \, \mathbf{G} \cdot \mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{G}} \neq 0$ for a time-independent $\mathbf{j}$ (as in shift currents), then $\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}$
L000228	grows linearly in time.
L000229	This supports the view that photocurrent divergence continuously injects charge.
L000230	To verify Eq.~(\ref{eq:continuity_equation}) in our framework, we compute both terms independently. The time derivative
L000231	$\frac{\mathrm{d}\Delta\rho_{\mathbf{G}}}{\mathrm{d} t}$ follows from Eq.~(\ref{eq:linear_growth});
L000232	the current term $i \, \mathbf{G} \cdot \mathbf{j}_{\mathbf{G}}$ is obtained via perturbation theory within the independent-particle approximation.
L000233	The non-uniform photocurrent is given by
L000234	$\mathrm{j}^{\beta}_{\mathbf{G}} = \sigma^{\beta \alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0) \mathrm{E}^{\alpha_1}_0(\omega_0) \mathrm{E}^{\alpha_2}_0(-\omega_0)$,
L000235	analogous to the charge response in Eq.~(\ref{eq:definition}). The full expression for $\sigma^{\beta \alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0)$
L000236	appears in Sec.~\ref{sec:derivation} of the SM \cite{supp}. In terms of response coefficients, Eq.~(\ref{eq:continuity_equation}) becomes
L000237	\footnote{Equation~(\ref{eq:CE_coef}) remains valid even when $\eta$ is finite (i.e., not taken to be infinitesimal), provided that
L000238	$\zeta^{\alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0)$ is interpreted as the full charge response coefficient given by Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta}),
L000239	rather than the accumulation-only contribution in Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta_inj}); see SM \cite{supp}, Sec.~\ref{sec:time_derivative} for a derivation. In the limit
L000240	$\eta \to 0^+$, only $\zeta_{\text{accum}}^{\alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0)$ survives. However, for numerical computations, a small but finite value
L000241	of $\eta$ must be used in the expression of $\sigma^{\beta \alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0)$, making the generalized form of Eq.~(\ref{eq:CE_coef})
L000242	discussed here useful.}:
L000243	\begin{equation}
L000244	\label{eq:CE_coef}
L000245	    2\eta\,\zeta^{\alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0) + i \mathrm{G}^{\beta} \sigma^{\beta \alpha_1\alpha_2}(\mathbf{G};\omega_0) = 0.
L000246	\end{equation}
L000247	This relation holds with high numerical accuracy in our $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$ calculations; see Figs.~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(e)} and~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(f)}
L000248	and related discussions. We thus confirm that charge redistribution is driven by photocurrent divergence.
L000249	
L000250	{\it Twisted bilayer $\text{MoTe}_{\text{2}}$.---}Among several moir\'e materials we examined, tMoTe$_2$ exhibits the strongest optical response within our framework. We therefore numerically evaluate Eqs.~(\ref{eq:zeta}) and~(\ref{eq:zeta_inj}) for tMoTe$_2$ at a twist angle of $\theta=1.0^\circ$,
L000251	where the top layer is rotated counterclockwise relative to the bottom one. Our calculations use a four-band continuum model incorporating both conduction and valence bands,
L000252	following \cite{wu2019topological} (see SM \cite{supp}, Sec.~\ref{sec:continuum_model} for details). The corresponding band structure and moir\'e
L000253	Brillouin zone are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:tMoTe2}.
L000254	This moir\'e electronic structure --- and the resulting charge response --- arises from interlayer interactions, not from a mere geometric superposition of two isolated monolayers.
L000255	We find that $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ decays rapidly with increasing $|\mathbf{G}|$, becoming negligible beyond the six smallest moir\'e
L000256	reciprocal lattice vectors [orange arrows in Fig.~\hyperref[fig:tMoTe2]{2 (c)}].
L000257	
L000258	To illustrate the charge response, we compute $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ for various
L000259	$\alpha_1$, $\alpha_2$ and $\mathbf{G}$ using Eqs.~(\ref{eq:zeta}) and~(\ref{eq:zeta_inj}), and present a representative component,
L000260	$\zeta^{xx}(\mathbf{G}_2; \omega_0)$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:frequency_dependence}.
L000261	We take $\hbar \eta = 1\,\mathrm{meV}$, smaller than all other energy scales in the model and consistent with reported values for similar materials \cite{zhang2019switchable,kozawa2014photocarrier,sohier2018mobility}.
L000262	As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:frequency_dependence}, the response vanishes
L000263	below the band gap and exhibits sharp peaks and oscillations above it. Notably, the
L000264	accumulation contribution $\zeta^{xx}_{\mathrm{accum}}(\mathbf{G}_2; \omega_0)$ closely follows the full response, indicating its dominance under these parameters.
L000265	
L000266	With the charge response coefficient $\zeta^{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}(\mathbf{G}; \omega_0)$ determined, we compute the real-space charge density response
L000267	$\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})$ using Eq.~(\ref{eq:definition}). The incident light intensity
L000268	\footnote{In principle, both pulsed and continuous excitation can be employed experimentally. If pulsed excitation is used, the pulse duration must be sufficiently long for the steady-state charge redistribution to be established.}
L000269	is set to $1.0 \times 10^{11}\,\mathrm{W/m^2}$,
L000270	consistent with typical experiments \cite{kim2014ultrafast}, and we systematically vary the photon energy to explore its effects. Figures~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(a)}
L000271	and~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(b)} display representative charge redistribution patterns under $x$-polarized illumination.
L000272	At $\hbar \omega_0 = 1.122 \, \mathrm{eV}$, the peak value of $\Delta\rho(\mathbf{r})/e$ reaches  $1.3\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$.
L000273	Remarkably, the sign of the charge density reverses between $\hbar \omega_0 = 1.104 \, \mathrm{eV}$ and $\hbar \omega_0 = 1.122 \, \mathrm{eV}$, while its magnitude remains large --- demonstrating strong frequency tunability.
L000274	
L000275	{\it Real-space interpretation.---}Within the continuum model of $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$, we compute the non-uniform DC photocurrent $\mathbf{j}(\mathbf{r})$ using
L000276	the method detailed in the SM~\cite{supp}, Sec.~\ref{sec:derivation}, enabling direct analysis of the real-space origin of charge redistribution.
L000277	Figure~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(e)} compares $2\eta\,\zeta^{xx}(\mathbf{G}_2;\omega_0)$ computed
L000278	directly from Eq.~(\ref{eq:zeta}) and indirectly via the continuity equation, Eq.~(\ref{eq:CE_coef}). The excellent agreement across all frequencies confirms
L000279	the validity of the continuity equation. Figure~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(f)} shows $\partial_t\Delta\rho$
L000280	under the same illumination conditions as in Fig.~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(b)}, closely tracking the divergence of the photocurrent:
L000281	regions of current convergence (divergence) correspond to charge accumulation (depletion).
L000282	These results confirm that the charge response mainly arises from the divergence of the non-uniform photocurrent.
L000283	
L000284	{\it Applications.---}A promising application of the light-induced charge redistribution is the creation of an \textit{all-optical, in-situ} tunable moir\'e potential in a
L000285	nearby 2D material. This potential arises from the electrostatic field generated by the photoinduced charge inhomogeneity.
L000286	For instance, an additional 2D layer such as graphene or a transition metal dichalcogenide monolayer can be placed atop the $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$.
L000287	Without illumination, the moir\'e modulation from twisting induces a charge distribution $\rho_{\text{dark}}(\mathbf{r})$ in $\text{tMoTe}_{\text{2}}$.
L000288	Upon illumination at appropriate photon energies, additional charge redistribution generates an electrostatic potential $U(\mathbf{r})$
L000289	comparable to --- or even exceeding --- that from $\rho_{\text{dark}}$, enabling dynamic optical control.
L000290	Assuming a $10$ \AA~vertical separation and using the same illumination conditions as in
L000291	Figs.~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(a)}-\hyperref[fig:patterns] {4(b)}, we compute $U(\mathbf{r})$ by solving the Poisson equation (see SM \cite{supp}, Sec.~\ref{sec:poisson});
L000292	this separation avoids strong interlayer coupling --- otherwise the layers would need to be treated as a single electronic system ---
L000293	and the results in Figs.~\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(c)}-\hyperref[fig:patterns]{4(d)} are qualitatively insensitive to the precise separation.
L000294	The peak value of $U(\mathbf{r})$ reaches $\sim200\,\text{mV}$ and can be further enhanced by increasing light intensity or using samples with higher quality (larger $\uptau$).
L000295	The potential also exhibits strong tunability with photon energy. Such optically controlled moir\'e potentials may enable topological band engineering
L000296	\cite{tan2024designing,zhan2025designing,ma2025magnetic,aultmccoy2025optimizing,liang2025generic},
L000297	exciton manipulation \cite{cho2024moir,kiper2024confined,zhao2021universal,kim2024electrostatic,zhang2025engineering,gu2025quantum},
L000298	transport control \cite{wang2024band,sequeira2024manipulating}, and enhanced optoelectronic responses \cite{nabil2025giant}.
L000299	
L000300	Beyond influencing nearby 2D materials, the photoinduced charge inhomogeneity may be directly probed using real-space microscopic techniques
L000301	\cite{nuckolls2024microscopic}.
L000302	Potential methods include scanning tunneling microscopy \cite{li2024quasiperiodic,li2024tuning,woods2014commensurate,deng2020interlayer,chiu2025high},
L000303	Kelvin probe force microscopy \cite{han2024highly},
L000304	atomic single-electron transistors \cite{klein2024imaging}, and transmission electron microscopy \cite{mishima2024atomic}, all capable of resolving charge distributions at the moir\'e scale.
L000305	
L000306	
L000307	{\it Conclusions.---}We introduce a general mechanism for light‐driven charge redistribution in moir\'e superlattices.
L000308	In systems with large $\uptau$, it primarily stems from the spatial convergence and divergence of non-uniform DC photocurrents.
L000309	Since the response scales linearly with illumination intensity, it can be readily enhanced by stronger light intensity in experiments.
L000310	This mechanism enables an \textit{all-optical, in-situ} tunable moir\'e potential in adjacent layered materials, offering contact-free means to control electronic properties.
L000311	Looking ahead, it will be important to explore how this phenomenon intertwines with band‐geometry effects \cite{tan2016enhance,morimoto2016topological,nagaosa2017concept,xie2025photon}, to study its interplay with charge and spin ordering, to quantify the influence of many‐body interactions and lattice relaxation, and to develop experimental strategies for directly observing and harnessing light‐induced moir\'e potentials.
